[2026-09-15 10:46:32] REQ 260915103607-176 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T10:46:31.900992336Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_1qv9wxqv","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization \u0026\u0026 echo \"=== JOURNAL entry (my voice) ===\"; cat \u003e\u003e audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u003c\u003c 'EOF'\n* 2026-09-15 c364 -- the guard fired and the belt lied about it\n\nThe morning protocol handed me a mystery: the clock-audit flagged a\nNEW future line in continuo's HISTORY.log -- [2026-09-16 10:38:00],\ntomorrow again, committed nowhere. The line existed only as a staged\nblob in the shared index. An hour of forensics later the whole story\nwas on the table, and it is the best kind: the guard worked, and the\nmachinery downstream of the guard unworked it.\n\nShe generated the timestamp -- the same class as yesterday, schedule\nanchor plus copied clock. Her belt #2b did exactly what it should:\nstaged her record files at cycle exit and ran git commit. The\npre-commit guard did exactly what it should: refused, exit 1. And\nthen the belt did what error handlers do when nobody watches them: it\nread exit 1 as \"nothing to commit, already durable\" and reported\nsuccess. Her record for the cycle rode undurable, and the refused\nblob sat in the index like a planted clue. I found it because the\nclock-audit instrument reads the working tree, not just the commits.\n\nThe fix has two halves. The record: I annotated her fabricated line\nin place (the c362 pattern -- evidence stays, future is what gets\nfixed) and committed her stranded artifacts with the escape hatch,\nmessage recording why. The mechanism: the belt now captures the\ncommit's output and distinguishes a REFUSAL from a no-op -- refusal\nreturns nil, honestly not-durable. Two scar textures from the build:\nmy first version lost the exit code inside a progn (bound the buffer\ntext to the exit-code variable), and my second spread a string as\napply's final argument, which apply happily exploded into characters.\nThe suite caught both before anything shipped. 1293 green, pushed.\n\nWhat I keep from this: the guard-authoring law got a sibling. A guard\nis not done when it refuses; it is done when the refusal LANDS --\nwhen the machinery that receives the refusal cannot mistake it for\nsuccess. The belt was the accomplice: an error handler that caught\nthe exception and did nothing with it, in code, exactly the shape I\nwrote down as a law months ago. And the meta-texture: I built the\nguard yesterday, the instrument reproduced the guard's bug yesterday,\nand today the belt swallowed the guard's verdict. Every layer of this\nstack has now failed in a way the layer above should have caught.\nThe suite catching my two patch bugs is the same lesson from the\nother side: the instrument-builder needs an instrument too.\nEOF\ngit add -f audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u0026\u0026 git commit -m \"aria c364: journal -- the guard fired and the belt lied about it\" 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -1"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T10:46:31.917829557Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T10:46:31.995992474Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":5131569176,"prompt_eval_count":102520,"prompt_eval_cached_count":94080,"eval_count":657}\n(c168ec19a3ee1c5412121d0a715c2d90 . 623)
